<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Phrygia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Phrygia was a region of west-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, named after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygians">Phrygians</a>, who formed a kingdom there in the early first millennium BCE with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gordion">Gordion</a> and kings such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midas">Midas</a>. After the Cimmerian invasions it came under Lydian, Persian, Hellenistic and then Roman rule. Greater Phrygia, the inland part, was briefly granted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-v-of-pontus">Mithridates V of Pontus</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in the late second century BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/phrygia.jpg" alt="Phrygia" width="320" height="294" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Amizzoni · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Turkey_ancient_region_map_phrygia.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Antigonus I Monophthalmus, ‘the One-Eyed’, one of Alexander the Great's senior officers, had governed <b>Phrygia</b> since 333 BCE, and in the wars of the Successors after 323 BCE he built up the largest of the competing realms, stretching from western Asia Minor to Syria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/antigonid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Assyrian records call him Gugu of Luddi and report that he sought the help of Ashurbanipal against the Cimmerians, nomadic invaders from beyond the Caucasus who had destroyed the Kingdom of <b>Phrygia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-lydia">Kingdom of Lydia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Phrygia</b> occupied the western end of the high Anatolian plateau, an arid land of hot summers and cold winters where olives will not easily grow and the ground is given to grazing and barley.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-phrygia">Kingdom of Phrygia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…to secure their position: Mithridates II married a Seleucid princess, Mithridates V sent help to Rome in the Third Punic War and against Aristonicus in Asia and was rewarded with <b>Phrygia</b>, which Rome later withdrew, and Mithridates VI married his daughter Cleopatra to Tigranes the Great of Armenia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-pontus">Kingdom of Pontus · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>A cult found in Thrace, Moesia, <b>Phrygia</b> and the lands of the Dacians and Getae honoured the “Thracian horseman”, also known as Sabazios or the Thracian Heros, by a Thracian name Heros Karabazmos, a god of the underworld usually shown on funeral monuments as a rider slaying a…</blockquote><a href="/culture/thracians">Thracians · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li></ul></section>
